The page finishes **6.2.4**: always monitor training and validation loss, plus training and validation values of every metric you care about. When validation performance starts to degrade, you have achieved overfitting.

**6.2.5 Regularizing and tuning your model.** With statistical power and the ability to overfit, you are on the right path, and the goal becomes **maximizing generalization performance**. This phase takes the most time: modify the model, train, evaluate on *validation* data (not test data), and repeat until the model is as good as it can get. Things to try:

- different architectures; add or remove layers;
- dropout;
- L1 or L2 regularization if the model is small;
- different hyperparameters (units per layer, optimizer learning rate);
- optionally, more data curation or feature engineering: collect and annotate more data, build better features, remove uninformative ones.

Much of this can be automated with **automated hyperparameter-tuning software** such as **KerasTuner** (chapter 18).

A caution: every time validation feedback guides a change, you **leak information about the validation process into the model**. A few times is harmless; done systematically over many iterations, the model ends up *overfitting to the validation process* even though it never trains on validation data, and evaluation becomes less reliable.

Once the configuration is satisfactory, **train the final production model on all available data (training + validation)** and evaluate it **one last time on the test set**. If test performance is significantly worse than validation performance, either the validation procedure was not reliable or you overfitted the validation data while tuning; consider a more reliable protocol such as **iterated K-fold validation**.

**6.3 Deploying your model** begins: after clearing the final test evaluation, the model is ready to be deployed and begin its productive life.
